Let me recheck the count. Multiples of 6 from 32 to 99 are 36, 42, ..., 96, i.e., 6·6 through 6·16. The number of terms is 16 − 6 + 1 = 11, not 10 — I forgot the "+1" in my earlier count.

Verification: (96 − 36)/6 + 1 = 60/6 + 1 = 11. The squares are 36², 42², 48², 54², 60², 66², 72², 78², 84², 90², 96² — eleven values, all four-digit and divisible by 6. So the earlier answer was off by one.

